<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-10-22">
  <title>Resources for 10/22</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of addition and multiplication of real numbers, where the real numbers are equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain potential ambiguities in the definitions of addition and multiplication of real numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove that addition and multiplication of real numbers is well defined.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given two real numbers, find the sum or product of the numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove the field axioms of real numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of the relation <m>&lt;</m> on the set of real numbers, where the real numbers are equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences of rational numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain potential ambiguities in the definitions of the relation <m>&lt;</m> on the set of real numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove that the relation <m>&lt;</m> is well defined on the set of real numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given two real numbers $a$ and $b$, determine whether <m>a &lt; b</m>, <m>b &lt; a</m>, or <m>a=b</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove the ordered field axioms of real numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-equiv-arithmetic-2">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Real Number Arithmetic</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on <em>real number arithmetic</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Real Number Arithmetic</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the set of all Cauchy sequences of rational numbers.  Let <m>\mathbb{R}</m> be the set of equivalence classes of <m>A</m> under the equivalence relation we defined previously.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m> (so <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are equivalence classes whose elements are Cauchy sequences of rational numbers).  How would you define <m>X+Y</m>?  Describe it as a process, and make sure that your answer is in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as defined above.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>.  How would you define <m>X \cdot Y</m>?  Describe it as a process, and make sure that your answer is in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as defined above.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose that <m>\{a_n\}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> are Cauchy sequences such that <m>[\{ a_n \}] = [\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}]</m> and <m>[\{ b_n \}] = [\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is your guess for <m>[\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}] \cdot [\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Suppose you use the sequences <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> as representatives of <m>[\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}]</m> and <m>[\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>.  Prove that <m>[\{ a_n \}] \cdot [\{ b_n \}]</m> is the same as your guess in part (a).
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain how the addition and multiplication defined in #1 and #2 could potentially be ambiguous.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Explain what you must assume and what you must prove in order for addition in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> to be well defined.  Then try proving that addition is well defined.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Prove as many of the field axioms as you can for <m>\mathbb{R}</m> with addition and multiplication defined above.  You may use the fact that <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is a field.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-arithmetic-order">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for the Order Relation on Real Numbers</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on the <em>order relation</em> on real numbers, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Order Relation on Real Numbers</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the set of all Cauchy sequences of rational numbers.  Let <m>\mathbb{R}</m> be the set of equivalence classes of <m>A</m> under the equivalence relation we defined previously.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider the real numbers <m>X = \left[ \left\{ 5-\frac{11}{n} \right\} \right]</m> and <m>Y = \left[ \left\{ 1+\frac{3}{n^2} \right\} \right]</m>.  Determine whether <m>X &lt; Y</m>, <m>Y &lt; X</m>, or <m>X=Y</m>.  Then prove it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Explain what you must assume and what you must prove in order for <m>&lt;</m> to be well defined.  Then try proving that <m>&lt;</m> is well defined.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Prove as many of the ordered field axioms that pertain to order as you can for <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.  You may use the fact that <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is an ordered field.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Addition and Multiplication of Real Numbers
    </p>
    <p>
      Order Relation on Real Numbers
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>